data.content:
`WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` was deprecated in Android 11 (API 30) and has been fully removed from .NET MAUI in .NET 10, which now defaults to **edge-to-edge** rendering.

Here's how to handle it in .NET 10:

## 1. Use `KeyboardAutoManagerScroll` (simplest)

MAUI includes automatic keyboard-avoidance scrolling. It's **on by default**. If you disabled it, re-enable it in your `MauiProgram.cs`:

```csharp
builder
    .UseMauiApp<App>()
    .ConfigureMauiHandlers(handlers =>
    {
#if ANDROID
        Microsoft.Maui.Platform.KeyboardAutoManagerScroll.Connect();
#endif
    });
```

Make sure you haven't called `KeyboardAutoManagerScroll.Disconnect()` elsewhere.

## 2. Handle insets manually (more control)

If auto-scroll isn't enough (e.g., a sticky bottom input bar), set padding based on the keyboard inset. In your `MainActivity.cs`:

```csharp
using Android.Views;
using AndroidX.Core.View;
using Microsoft.Maui.ApplicationModel;

// In OnCreate, after base.OnCreate:
ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(
    Window!.DecorView, new KeyboardInsetsListener());

// Listener class:
class KeyboardInsetsListener : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
{
    public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(
        Android.Views.View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
    {
        var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
        var navInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.NavigationBars());

        int bottomPadding = Math.Max(imeInsets.Bottom, navInsets.Bottom);

        // Update the page's padding on the UI thread
        Platform.CurrentActivity?
            .FindViewById(Android.Resource.Id.Content)?
            .SetPadding(0, 0, 0, bottomPadding);

        return ViewCompat.OnApplyWindowInsets(v, insets);
    }
}
```

## 3. Quick fix: revert to legacy mode (not recommended long-term)

In `MainActivity.cs`, set the soft input mode to `AdjustPan`:

```csharp
protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
{
    base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);
    Window!.SetSoftInputMode(SoftInput.AdjustPan);
}
```

This scrolls the whole window up rather than resizing — it works but can clip top content.

---

**Recommendation:** Start with option **1** (auto-scroll). If your layout has a sticky bottom bar that doesn't cooperate, use option **2** with the `WindowInsets` listener for precise control.
